Consider Boolean networks defined as follows. A Boolean network on $n$ variables is a function $F:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}^n$, with $F(x)=(f_1(x),\ldots,f_n(x))$ and each $f_i:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}$. Under the asynchronous update scheme, the dynamics on the state space $\{0,1\}^n$ is given by the transition relation in which, from a state $x$, one chooses any index $i$ with $f_i(x)\ne x_i$ and moves to the state obtained from $x$ by flipping coordinate $i$ (states with $F(x)=x$ are fixed points). An attractor is a minimal terminal strongly connected component of this asynchronous transition graph; an attractor that is not a single fixed point is called complex. A subspace (or subcube) of $\{0,1\}^n$ is a set obtained by fixing some coordinates to $0$ or $1$ and letting the others vary. A trap space is a subspace $S$ that is invariant under the asynchronous dynamics: no transition leaves $S$. A minimal trap space is a trap space that contains no strictly smaller trap space. A motif-avoidant attractor (MAA) is a complex asynchronous attractor whose states do not lie in any minimal trap space.

Provide a rigorous explanation, with quantitative bounds and a computationally efficient characterization, of why motif-avoidant attractors are extremely rare under asynchronous update across the following regimes simultaneously:
(a) Boolean networks arising as models of real biological regulatory systems;
(b) random Boolean networks with sparse in-degree distributions;
(c) random Boolean networks with dense in-degree distributions;
(d) ensembles in which the local update functions $f_i$ exhibit input-node canalization (i.e., for each $i$ there exist inputs $j$ and values $v\in\{0,1\}$ such that $x_j=v$ forces the value of $f_i$).

A complete answer should yield necessary and sufficient structural conditions on $(f_1,\ldots,f_n)$ for the existence of a motif-avoidant attractor that can be checked in time polynomial in $n$ (or otherwise efficiently), together with probabilistic estimates showing vanishing prevalence of MAAs in the random ensembles above.

